## 7. DEPLOYMENT PROCEDURES

### 7.1 Traditional WAR/JAR Deployment (Tomcat)

Step 1: Build Application
```bash
cd /home/deployer/myweb
git pull origin master
mvn clean package
```

Step 2: Backup Current Version
```bash
cp /opt/tomcat/webapps/myweb.war /backup/myweb-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S).war
```

Step 3: Stop Tomcat
```bash
/opt/tomcat/bin/shutdown.sh
```

Step 4: Deploy New Version
```bash
cp target/myweb-1.0.0.war /opt/tomcat/webapps/myweb.war
```

Step 5: Update Configuration (if needed)
```bash
cp config/application-prod.properties /opt/tomcat/conf/myweb.properties
```

Step 6: Start Tomcat
```bash
/opt/tomcat/bin/startup.sh
```

Step 7: Verify Deployment
```bash
# Check logs
tail -f /opt/tomcat/logs/catalina.out

# Health check
curl https://prod-server.example.com/api/health

# Verify functionality
# ... perform smoke tests
```

### 7.2 Spring Boot JAR Deployment

Step 1: Create Deployment Directory
```bash
mkdir -p /opt/myweb/{app,logs,config}
cd /opt/myweb
```

Step 2: Copy Application
```bash
cp /home/deployer/myweb/target/myweb-1.0.0.jar app/
cp config/application-prod.properties config/
```

Step 3: Create systemd Service File
```bash
# /etc/systemd/system/myweb.service

[Unit]
Description=MyWeb Application
After=network.target

[Service]
Type=simple
User=appuser
WorkingDirectory=/opt/myweb
ExecStart=/usr/bin/java -Xmx512m -jar app/myweb-1.0.0.jar \
    --spring.config.location=/opt/myweb/config/application-prod.properties
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=10

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
```

Step 4: Enable and Start Service
```bash
sudo systemctl daemon-reload
sudo systemctl enable myweb
sudo systemctl start myweb
sudo systemctl status myweb
```

Step 5: Monitor Application
```bash
# Check service status
sudo systemctl status myweb

# View logs
sudo journalctl -u myweb -f

# Check if running
ps aux | grep myweb
```

### 7.3 Blue-Green Deployment

Preparation:
  1. Prepare two identical production servers (Blue & Green)
  2. Blue: Current production version
  3. Green: New version (ready to deploy)

Deployment Steps:
  1. Deploy new version to Green server
  2. Run smoke tests on Green
  3. If tests pass:
     - Switch load balancer traffic to Green
     - Monitor Green for issues
     - Keep Blue as rollback option
  4. If issues detected:
     - Switch back to Blue
     - Fix issues
     - Redeploy to Green

Benefits:
  ✓ Zero downtime deployment
  ✓ Easy rollback
  ✓ Full environment testing before cutover

